      "text": "I can’t honestly give **genome-personalized** recommendations in this session because the GenomeClaw plugin tools aren’t exposed here, and web search is also disabled. I checked memory and found no prior fitness/diet synthesis. So below is an **interim evidence-based plan**, plus the exact genome panel I would query once GenomeClaw is reachable.\n\n## What I would check for true personalization\n\nFor muscle-building and diet personalization, I would query:\n\n**Training / muscle response**\n- **ACTN3** — fast-twitch power/sprint signal; useful but small effect for general fitness.\n- **ACE** — endurance vs power association; inconsistent but sometimes relevant.\n- **AMPD1** — exercise intolerance / fatigue signal in some carriers.\n- **MSTN, IGF1, VDR, COL1A1/COL5A1** — muscle/tendon adaptation and injury-risk context; mostly small-effect/heterogeneous.\n- **CKM, PPARGC1A** — energy metabolism/endurance adaptation.\n\n**Diet / appetite / metabolic response**\n- **FTO, MC4R** — appetite/body-weight tendency; not destiny, but useful for satiety strategy.\n- **TCF7L2, PPARG, IRS1, KCNJ11** — glycemic/metabolic context.\n- **APOE, LPA, LDLR/APOB/PCSK9** — lipid-response and cardiovascular diet framing.\n- **LCT** — lactose tolerance.\n- **CYP1A2 + ADORA2A** — caffeine metabolism/anxiety/sleep response.\n- **ALDH2/ADH1B** — alcohol metabolism.\n- **MTHFR** — folate-cycle context, usually over-marketed unless a clear high-impact genotype is present.\n\n**PRS attempts I’d normally run**\n- Strength/lean-mass PRS: no single clinical-grade PRS I’d treat as strongly actionable.\n- **BMI/body-weight PRS** if available.\n- **Type 2 diabetes PRS**, e.g. PGS000014 if supported.\n- **Coronary artery disease PRS**, e.g. PGS000018 if diet/lipid risk is part of the question.\n\n## Interim training plan for building muscle\n\nFor general fitness hypertrophy, I’d use the plan below regardless of genotype, then personalize once variants are available.\n\n**1. Lift 3–4 days/week.**  \nUse full-body or upper/lower split. Prioritize compound lifts: squat or leg press, hinge/deadlift variant, bench/push-up, row, overhead press, pulldown/pull-up.\n\n**2. Weekly volume: 10–20 hard sets per muscle group.**  \nStart near 10–12 sets/week, then increase only if recovery is good. More is not always better.\n\n**3. Rep ranges: mix 5–10 and 8–15.**  \nMost hypertrophy comes from hard sets close to failure, not a magic rep range. Keep 1–3 reps in reserve for most sets; occasionally push isolation lifts closer to failure.\n\n**4. Progression rule.**  \nWhen you can hit the top of the rep range on all sets with good form, increase load 2.5–5%.\n\n**5. Recovery guardrails.**\n- Sleep: 7.5–9 hours if possible.\n- Deload every 4–8 weeks if performance stalls or joints ache.\n- If soreness lasts >72 hours or lifts regress for 2+ sessions, reduce volume before adding supplements.\n\n**6. Cardio without compromising muscle.**  \nDo 2–3 sessions/week of zone 2 cardio, 20–40 minutes. Keep intense intervals modest if hypertrophy is the priority.\n\nEvidence basis: protein and resistance-training meta-analyses support progressive resistance training plus adequate protein for lean-mass gain; see Morton et al. 2018 on protein supplementation and resistance training [PMID 28698222](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28698222/) and Schoenfeld et al. on dose-response training volume [PMID 27433992](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27433992/).\n\n## Interim diet plan\n\n**1. Protein: 1.6–2.2 g/kg/day.**  \nFor muscle gain, aim near **1.6 g/kg/day** as the default; go toward 2.0–2.2 g/kg/day if cutting, older, very lean, or appetite allows.\n\n**2. Calories: small surplus if gaining.**\n- Muscle gain: +150 to +300 kcal/day.\n- Recomposition: maintenance calories with high protein and progressive lifting.\n- Fat loss while preserving muscle: −300 to −500 kcal/day, high protein, keep lifting heavy.\n\n**3. Carbs around training.**  \nFor performance, place more carbs pre/post workout: rice, potatoes, oats, fruit, whole grains. If future genotype/PRS suggests higher glycemic risk, I’d bias carbs toward high-fiber, lower-glycemic sources rather than removing them entirely.\n\n**4. Fat quality.**  \nUse olive oil, nuts, avocado, fatty fish; limit trans fats and keep saturated fat moderate. If APOE or lipid-related findings are relevant, this may become more specific.\n\n**5. Fiber: 25–40 g/day.**  \nThis helps satiety, lipids, and glucose regulation. If FTO/MC4R appetite-risk variants are present, high-fiber/high-protein meals become especially useful.\n\n**6. Creatine.**  \nCreatine monohydrate 3–5 g/day is one of the few supplements with strong evidence for strength/power support. Hydrate normally; avoid treating it as a substitute for training consistency.\n\n**7. Caffeine experiment.**  \nUntil CYP1A2/ADORA2A are known: try caffeine only before noon for two weeks, track sleep latency and workout quality. If you’re a slow metabolizer or anxiety-sensitive, the recommendation would likely become stricter.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nRight now, I can give a strong **non-genomic baseline**: lift progressively 3–4x/week, target 10–20 hard sets per muscle/week, eat 1.6–2.2 g/kg/day protein, keep calories aligned to your goal, and use creatine if tolerated.  \n\nFor a truly personalized version, I need live GenomeClaw access to check ACTN3, ACE, AMPD1, FTO, MC4R, TCF7L2, APOE, CYP1A2, ADORA2A, LCT, lipid genes, and relevant PRS results.",
